# PRR fast retransmit (RFC 6937): pure dup-ACKs trigger fast recovery.
# Under PRR, cwnd is NOT collapsed to 1*MSS (that was Tahoe); instead
# ssthresh = max(FlightSize/2, 2*MSS) and a per-ACK snd_credit paces
# the retransmission.
#
# NB: this script uses wscale=0 on the peer side to keep the post-
# handshake window comparison straightforward. With WS negotiated, the
# SYN-ACK window is unscaled (RFC 7323 §2.3) but post-handshake windows
# are scaled — the first dup-ACK then differs from the cached snd_wnd
# and gets ignored by the "window unchanged" dup-ACK filter. That's
# correct behaviour but makes scripted dup-ACK timing brittle.

--connect 10.0.0.1:49152 10.0.0.2:80
+0 > SEW 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460, wscale 5, sackOK, TS val * ecr 0>
+.005 < S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 32768 <mss 1460, wscale 0, sackOK, TS val 1 ecr *>
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>

# Push one MSS of data (effective MSS after TS option = 1448 bytes).
--send 1448
+0 > P. 1:1449(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>

# Three pure dup-ACKs at ack=1 with unchanged window fire fast retransmit.
+.005 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val 2 ecr *>
+.001 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val 3 ecr *>
+.001 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val 4 ecr *>

# PRR retransmits one segment under the initial snd_credit=MSS budget.
+0 > P. 1:1449(1448) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 4>
